  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does the calf muscle pump blood?",
      "a": "When the calf muscle contracts, it squeezes the deep veins running through it, pushing blood upwards. It is sometimes called the body's second heart.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the calf muscle",
        "What is the muscle pump?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do veins have valves?",
      "a": "Blood in veins is under low pressure and in the legs must flow uphill. One-way valves let blood move towards the heart and close if it starts to fall back.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the upper valve",
        "What do vein valves do?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do legs feel stiff after standing still for a long time?",
      "a": "Without walking, the calf pump isn't working, so blood pools in the leg veins and fluid can seep into the tissues. Moving restarts the pump.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do feet swell when standing still?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Do arteries have valves?",
      "a": "No, apart from the valves at the exits of the heart. Arteries carry blood under high pressure from the heart's beat, so they don't need them; veins carry low-pressure blood and do.",
      "asks": [
        "Why don't arteries need valves?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What else helps blood return to the heart?",
      "a": "Breathing helps too: when you breathe in, pressure in the chest falls, drawing blood up into the big veins near the heart.",
      "asks": [
        "How does breathing help circulation?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Leg veins contain one-way valves that stop blood flowing backwards.",
    "The calf muscle pump is often called the second heart."
  ],
